{
  "title": "Terrible Triad Injury of Elbow",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/trauma/1021/terrible-triad-injury-of-elbow",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Feb 24 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Terrible Triad Injury of Elbow is a traumatic injury pattern of the elbow characterized by elbow dislocation, radial head/neck fracture, and a coronoid fracture.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can be made with plain radiographs of the elbow. CT studies are helpful for surgical planning.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is generally ORIF versus radial head arthroplasty, LCL reconstruction, coronoid ORIF, and possible MCL reconstruction.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Characterized by presence of",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1021/images/screen-shot-2016-01-26-at-1.40.40-pm.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**elbow dislocation** (often associated with posterolateral dislocation or LCL injury )",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**radial head or neck fracture**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**coronoid fracture**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "fall on supinated forearm and extended arm that results in a combination of",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "valgus, axial, and posterolateral rotatory forces",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "produces **posterolateral dislocation**",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "pathoanatomy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "structures of elbow fail from lateral to medial",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "LCL disrupted first",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "anterior capsule injured next",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "possible MCL disruption",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radial head",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "a secondary restraint to posterolateral rotatory instability (PLRI)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "secondary valgus stabilizer",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "forearm in neutral rotation, lateral portion of articular margin devoid of cartilage",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "roughly between radial styloid and listers tubercle",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Coronoid process",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "provides an anterior and varus buttress to ulnohumeral joint",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "resists posterior subluxation beyond 30 deg of flexion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "fracture fragment typically has some anterior capsule attached",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "useful in repair",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Medial collateral ligament",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1021/images/amcl.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "three components",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "anterior bundle",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "most important to stability, restraint to valgus and posteromedial rotatory instability",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "inserts on sublime tubercle (anteromedial facet of coronoid)",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "specifically inserts 18.4mm dorsal to tip of coronoid process",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "2",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "posterior bundle",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "transverse ligament",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Lateral collateral ligament",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1021/images/lcl.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            },
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1021/images/Figure%201%20-%20Ligaments%20copy_moved.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "inserts on supinator crest distal to lesser sigmoid notch",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "the primary restraint to posterolateral rotatory instability",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "four components",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**lateral ulnar collateral ligament (**most important for stability)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**radial collateral ligament**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**annular ligament**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**accessory collateral ligament**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "when injured is usually avulsed off of the lateral epicondyle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "patients complain of pain, clicking and locking with elbow in extension",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "possible varus / valgus instability patterns",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "distal radial ulnar joint must be evaluated for possible Essex-Lopresti injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1021/images/xcc.jpg",
              "caption": "terrible triad of the elbow"
            },
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/1021/images/xc.jpg",
              "caption": "terrible triad of the elbow"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "evaluate for concentricity of ulnohumeral and radiocapitellar joints",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "line drawn through center of radial neck should intersect the center of the capitellum regardless of radiographic projection",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "evaluate lateral radiograph for coronoid fracture",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "need prereduction and postredcution films",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "consider PA and lateral films of wrist and forearm when indicated",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CT",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "often utilized for better evaluation of coronoid fracture",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "3D imaging for determining fracture line propagation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "immobilization in 90 deg of flexion for 7-10 days",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications (rare)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "ulnohumeral and radiocapitellar joints must be concentrically reduced",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "radial head fx must not meet surgical indications",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "coronoid fx must be small",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "elbow should be sufficiently stable to allow early ROM",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "techniques",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "one week of immobilization followed by progressive ROM",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "active motion initiated with resting splint at 90 degrees and forearm pronation, avoiding terminal extension",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "static progressive extension splinting at night after 4-6 weeks",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "strengthening protocol after 6 weeks",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "ORIF versus radial head arthroplasty, LCL reconstruction, coronoid ORIF, possible MCL reconstruction",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "terrible triad elbow injury that includes an unstable radial head fracture, a type III coronoid fracture, and an associated elbow dislocation",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**coronoid fractures involving less than 10%** of the coronoid do not confer elbow stability in cadaveric studies and therefore **do not require repair**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "should instability persist after addressing the radial head and the LCL complex in the presence of a small coronoid fracture, the next best step is MCL reconstruction",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Techniques",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**ORIF vs replacement of radial head, coronoid ORIF, LCL reconstruction, and possible MCL reconstruction**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "approach",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "posterior skin incision advantageous",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "allows access to both medial and lateral aspect of elbow",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "lower risk of injury to cutaneous nerves",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "more cosmetic",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "technique",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "radial head ORIF vs. arthroplasty",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "radial head ORIF indicated if non comminuted fractures that involve < 40% articular surface",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "1.5, 2.0, or 2.4mm countersunk screws",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "plate if necessary; 2.0 plates cause minimal loss of motion even when placed on radial neck",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "plate position should be posterolateral",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": [
                            {
                              "text": "safe zone: 90-110 arc from radial styloid to Lister's tubercle with arm in neutral rotation",
                              "table": null,
                              "images": [],
                              "importance": "4",
                              "children": []
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "radial head arthroplasty indicated for comminuted radial head fxs (> 3 pieces)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "implant should articulate 2mm distal to the tip of the coronoid process",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "radial head resection without replacement is **NOT** indicated in presence of Essex-Lopresti lesion or in ligamentously injured elbows",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "if <25% head damaged or fragments not reconstructable and nonarticulating, can excise fractured portion if elbow stable (rarely indicated)",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "coronoid ORIF",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "can be fixed through radial head defect laterally",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "sutures, suture anchors, screws, or rarely plate fixation.",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "suture passed through 2 drill holes",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "posterior to anterior lag screws if fragment large",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "basal coronoid fxs (rare) fixed with anteromedial or medial plate on proximal ulna",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": [
                            {
                              "text": "FCU split approach preferred for rare isolated coronoid fractures",
                              "table": null,
                              "images": [],
                              "importance": "5",
                              "children": []
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "LCL repair",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "usually avulsed from origin on lateral epicondyle",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "reattach with suture anchors or transosseous sutures",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "must be reattached at center of capitellar curvature on lateral epicondyle",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "if MCL is intact, LCL is repaired with forearm in pronation",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "if MCL injured, LCL is repaired with forearm in supination to avoid medial gapping due to overtightening",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "repairs are performed with elbow at 90 degrees of flexion",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "MCL repair",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "indicated if instability on exam after LCL and fracture fixation, especially with extension beyond 30 degrees",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "persistent posteromedial instability following radial head replacement/repair and LCL repair in the setting of a Type I/II coronoid fracture should be managed with MCL repair",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "postoperative",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "elbow fixators - hinged or static",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "consider when instability is noted after complete bone and soft tissue repair",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "immobilization",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "can immobilize elbow in flexion with forearm pronation to provide stability against posterior subluxation",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "if both MCL and LCL were repaired, splint in flexion and neutral rotation",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "rehabilitation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "initiate active ROM exercises 48 hours after surgery to improve functional outcomes",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "if LCL is repaired, passive elbow motion should occur with the forearm in pronation",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "if MCL is repaired, passive elbow motion should occur with the forearm in supination",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "if both the MCL and LCL are repaired, passive elbow motion should occur with the forearm in neutral",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Complications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Instability**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "more common following type I or II coronoid fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Failure of internal fixation**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "most common following repair of radial neck fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "poor vascularity leading to osteonecrosis and nonunion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Post-traumatic stiffness**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "very common complication",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "initiate early ROM to prevent",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Heterotopic ossification**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "consider prophylaxis in pts with head injury or in setting of revision surgery",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Post-traumatic arthritis**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "due to chondral damage at time of injury and/or residual instability",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
